From: Chokri Hammedi <52697989+blue0x1@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] graphics: batch buffer syncs before address-reading shaders

Before a shader that reads guest memory by address, PrepareBda uploads
every range the CPU wrote since the last one. It did each buffer with its
own SynchronizeBuffer: a render-pass break and two full barriers apiece.
DREDGE writes ~330 cached buffers per frame this way.

Collect the ranges first, then do one staging upload, one render-pass
break and one barrier pair, then every copy. The perf log counts the
batches as "buffer-sync-batch" (work = buffers).

---
diff --git a/src/common/perfTrace.h b/src/common/perfTrace.h
index 090c906e8de7a08866da4fa33ef2fbb9526217ec..6e1e1b6705d05951836dfab2f961aa4d4f0ff7ff 100644
--- a/src/common/perfTrace.h
+++ b/src/common/perfTrace.h
@@ -32,6 +32,7 @@ enum Site : int {
 	WatchImageOn,
 	WatchImageOff,
 	StreamUpload,
+	BufferSyncBatch,
 	Count
 };
 
@@ -41,7 +42,7 @@ inline constexpr std::array<const char*, Count> Names {
     "image-via-buffer-down", "image-via-buffer-up", "image-copy-other", "draw-feedback-copy",
     "buffer-staging-copy", "buffer-download", "buffer-copy", "clear-color",
     "protect-buffer", "unprotect-buffer", "protect-image", "unprotect-image",
-    "stream-upload"};
+    "stream-upload", "buffer-sync-batch"};
 
 inline std::array<std::atomic<uint64_t>, Count> g_count {};
 inline std::array<std::atomic<uint64_t>, Count> g_work {};
